\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {I.1}{\ignorespaces Elements of proof for a global warming. Panel A shows the change in global surface temperature relative to the average of 1850-1900, as reconstructed (1-2000) and observation data (1850-2020). Panel B is a zoom of panel A with observed and simulated data, using human \& natural and only natural factors \citep {masson-delmotte_summary_2021}. Panel C present the top ten of the warmer years in global average (source: \url {https://www.climatecentral.org/}). Panel D is the global air surface temperature yearly evolution of the three years of my PhD, compared to 1992, the year of the third Earth summit when the United Nations committed to protect the future generations (data from C3S/ECMWF, ERA5, Copernicus: \url {https://pulse.climate.copernicus.eu/}).}}{7}{figure.caption.4}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {I.2}{\ignorespaces The "Keeling Curve": this graph illustrates the evolution of atmospheric \ce {CO2} concentration over the last two thousand years. Named after Charles D. Keeling who initiated the measurement project of continuous \ce {CO2} measurement at Mauna Loa observatory (Hawaii, U.S.) in 1958 \citep {keeling_atmospheric_2017}, the data is completed by ice core measurements \citep {rubino_revised_2019}. The industrial revolution is clearly highlighted by the dramatic increase that started in the late 18{th} century. Data can be found here: \url {https://keelingcurve.ucsd.edu/}.}}{9}{figure.caption.5}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {I.3}{\ignorespaces Historical \ce {CO2} emissions by regions and activities (A), put into perspective with the evolution of primary energy consumption by source. Data from \textit {Our World in Data}: \url {https://ourworldindata.org/}.}}{10}{figure.caption.6}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {I.4}{\ignorespaces Trend from implemented policies (red line) compared to emissions scenarios that would allow to stay below 1.5°C (cyan line) of below 2.0°C (green line) of global warming. A scenario that takes into account the National Determined Contributions (NDCs) discussed during COP26 is also represented (dark blue line). The NDCs represent the free of result commitments made by countries to reduce GHG emissions and enhance resilience to climate change impacts. Graphics from \citet {intergovernemental_panel_on_climate_change_climate_2023}}}{11}{figure.caption.7}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {I.5}{\ignorespaces Graphical abstract of the key aspects of the IPCC Assessment Report 6, Technical Summary. The upper panels present temperature projections for the five SSPs and three ranges of ECS. The lower panels show the responses of two climate indexes for three levels of warming. The association of upper and lower panels allows to catch a glimpse of the timeline for future global warming consequences. Graphics from \citet {intergovernemental_panel_on_climate_change_climate_2023}}}{14}{figure.caption.8}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {III.1}{\ignorespaces Overview of the tracer emissions in WRF-Chem and concentration series reconstructions. Regions of emissions of sea ice \textbf {(a)}, open-ocean \textbf {(b)}, and continental \textbf {(c)} tracers. \textbf {(d)} Example of reconstructed series of daily concentrations interpolated at Ny-Ålesund. \textbf {(e)} Map of the Arctic with the locations of the five studied stations. Base map from Cartopy {\copyright }~British Crown copyright 2016.}}{27}{figure.caption.11}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {III.2}{\ignorespaces Application of the ratio method as it is depicted in Sect.~\ref {P1sec:ratio_method}. Panel \textbf {(a)} shows the reconstructed daily concentration series of the continental tracer interpolated at Ny-Ålesund over the 2 years of simulation. Panel \textbf {(b)} shows the climatology of the total air mass origins; panel \textbf {(c)} shows the climatology of the origins of air masses that brought the 10\,{\%} highest concentrations; and panel \textbf {(d)} shows the ratio of \textbf {(c)} to \textbf {(b)}, noted as $R_\text {h}^{10}$.}}{32}{figure.caption.14}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {III.3}{\ignorespaces Application of the improved ratio method. Similarly to Fig.~\ref {P1fig:basic_method}, \textbf {(a)} shows the reconstructed daily concentration series of the continental tracer interpolated at Ny-Ålesund with the comparison of the standard sorting (top) and the seasonal sorting (bottom). Panel \textbf {(b)} is the climatology of the total air mass origins; \textbf {(c)} is the climatology of the origins of air masses that brought the 10\,{\%} highest concentrations; and \textbf {(d)} is the ratio of \textbf {(c)} to \textbf {(b)}, noted as $R_\text {h}^{10}$. In \textbf {(b)} and \textbf {(c)}, the 2\,{\%} lowest FPES values have been removed, as explained in Sect.~\ref {P1sec:improved_method}.}}{34}{figure.caption.15}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {III.4}{\ignorespaces Comparison of the results obtained with the standard ratio method \textbf {(a)} and with the improved ratio method \textbf {(b)}. Every panel presents the detection results for a specific tracer and for the five stations. The bars represent the contributions of each surface type to the detection (light blue for sea ice, blue for open ocean, green for continent). For every pair of tracer and station, the level of success is shown above the corresponding bars.}}{35}{figure.caption.16}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {III.5}{\ignorespaces Results of the combination of the five stations. Panel \textbf {(a)} shows the fraction of the contribution of every surface type for the three tracers and the associated success level. Panels \textbf {(b)}, \textbf {(c)}, and \textbf {(d)} are the composite ratio maps for the sea ice tracer, the open-ocean tracer, and the continental tracer, respectively.}}{38}{figure.caption.19}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {III.6}{\ignorespaces Panel of FPES visualizations and analysis for the sea ice tracer at the Tiksi station. Panels \textbf {(a)} and \textbf {(b)} are the climatologies of, respectively, the total FPES value and the values associated with the 10\,{\%} highest observations ($S_\text {h}^{10}$). Panels \textbf {(c)} and \textbf {(d)} are the ratio maps of the two versions of the ratio method ($R_\text {h}^{10}$ for, respectively, the standard and improved versions). Panel \textbf {(e)} is the quantification of the detection results for the climatology associated with the highest observation and for both versions of the ratio method.}}{40}{figure.caption.20}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {III.7}{\ignorespaces Evolution of the success level sum for different levels of low FPES filtering. In \textbf {(a)}, the sum is performed based on the scores of the five studied stations (Alert, Ny-Ålesund, Tiksi, Utqiaġvik, Villum) independently for the three tracers (sea ice, open ocean, continent). The total success level is shown as a black line. Panel \textbf {(b)} takes only the success levels superior or equal to 2 and sets them to 1. Dashed lines represent the theoretical maxima of the individual tracer scores (gray) and of the total score (black).}}{42}{figure.caption.21}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {III.8}{\ignorespaces Results of the improved ratio method when applied to a data series of absorption coefficients measured at the Zeppelin Observatory (Ny-Ålesund, Svalbard). Panel \textbf {(a)} is the ratio of the improved method that highlights the likely regions of origin. Panel \textbf {(b)} is the quantification of the surface type contributions to the three sources (sea ice, open ocean, continent).}}{43}{figure.caption.22}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {III.9}{\ignorespaces Results of the improved ratio method when applied to the data series of methanesulfonic acid (MSA) measured at Zeppelin Observatory. Panel \textbf {(a)} is the measurement series of MSA over 2020. The points corresponding to the 10\,{\%} strongest anomalies are represented in orange (high anomaly) and light-blue (low anomaly) squares. Panels \textbf {(b)} and \textbf {(c)} show the results for the complete series of measurements (January to December~2020). Panels \textbf {(d)} and \textbf {(e)} show the results for a period of continuous measurements of the dataset (April to early July).}}{48}{figure.caption.23}%
